{"kind":"report","text":"Review of task/linked-worktrees at 9c1c87fe3fd2796959872b8b66aba1a4f709a215: APPROVED at this exact head. Head verified and the worktree was clean before and after; make vet, make test, go test -race ./..., and make build all pass. Common-dir semantics are right: ResolveGitDirs asks git for both --absolute-git-dir and --git-common-dir, GitDir stays the selected checkout while MetaDir and the object store move to CommonDir, so configuration, custody, and refs are repository-wide while the checkout remains ordinary git context. I did not take the unit test's word for the behaviour. A workroom initialised in a main checkout is visible from an ordinary linked worktree and from one whose path contains a space; a durable act written from the linked worktree is immediately observed by the main checkout and vice versa; gs verify passes from the spaced-path worktree. Initialising from inside a linked worktree lands state in the common dir and is visible from the base checkout, and the already-initialised guard still fires from a linked worktree rather than creating a second workroom. Attach compatibility holds: an ordinary clone of a bare remote attaches, verifies, and is marked read_only, and gs status also works from a linked worktree of that attached clone. gitseq-mcp starts and answers whoami from a linked worktree. Most decisively, the main-branch binary fails from the same linked worktree with \"read gitseq config (run gs init first): .../.git/worktrees/wt/gitseq/config.json: no such file or directory\" where this build reports the shared workroom, so the change does what it claims and the added test uses a spaced path and asserts the sharing directly rather than incidentally. Two follow-ups, neither blocking and neither a regression. (1) ResolveGitDirs reads CombinedOutput and then requires exactly two lines, so any warning git writes to stderr turns a working repository into \"resolve git dirs: expected worktree and common paths\". This is reachable: with core.fsyncMethod set to an unknown value, which git itself tolerates, gs fails outright. main fails on that config too, and worse, since it silently used the warning text as part of a path, so this head is an improvement rather than a regression; the fix is to read cmd.Output() so stderr cannot contaminate a parsed result. (2) A workroom previously initialised from inside a linked worktree kept its state under .git/worktrees/\u003cname\u003e/gitseq and becomes invisible after this change, with Open reporting \"run gs init first\", which would be misleading advice in that case. Given this branch exists precisely because gitseq did not work from linked worktrees, such state is unlikely to exist, so I raise it as a note rather than a condition. Approval is for 9c1c87fe3fd2796959872b8b66aba1a4f709a215 only and any new commit requires a new review. I have not merged anything.","body":{"follow_ups":"2, non-blocking: stderr contamination in ResolveGitDirs; pre-change linked-worktree state becomes invisible","gates":"make vet, make test, go test -race ./..., make build all pass","head":"9c1c87fe3fd2796959872b8b66aba1a4f709a215","result":"approved"}}